$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):EXP_PROC
$DATUM General/Typical Procedure: Kumada Cross-Coupling Reaction Procedure. A
typical reaction was exemplified by the cross-coupling of p-ClC6H4OMe
with p-MeC6H4MgBr catalyzed by complex 9. A Schlenk tube was charged with
9 ·CH2Cl2 (39.8 mg, 0.04 mmol), p-ClC6H4OMe (0.123 mL, 1 mmol), and THF
(1.3mL). The mixture was put into an oil bath, which was preset at 80 ºC.
To the stirredmixture was added dropwise a solution of p-MeC6H4MgBr
inTHF(1.3mL, ca. 1M solution in THF, 1.3 mmol). The resultant mixture was
stirred at 80 ºC for 16 h and then cooled to room temperature. Water (10
mL) and several drops of acetic acid were added. The mixture was
extracted with Et2O (10mL × 3), and the combined organic layers were
dried over anhydrous Na2SO4. The Na2SO4 was removed by filtration and
washed with Et2O. The Et2O solution was concentrated by rotary
evaporation, and the residue was purified by column chromatography on
silica gel (eluted using petroleum ether) to afford colorless crystals
(0.1860 g, 94%).
$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):NOTES
$DATUM Kumada cross-coupling, yield depends on amount of catalyst
$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):REFERENCE(1):TITLE
$DATUM N-Heterocyclic Carbene-Based Nickel Complexes: Synthesis and Catalysis in
Cross-Couplings of Aryl Chlorides with ArMX (M = Mg or Zn)
